art (optionally driving the player), tiled into one PNG.  frames=<integer> every=<integer> columns=<integer> width=<integer> height=<integer> move=<array\n  play       A games/ game's HUD, outcome and status.  bot=<boolean> ticks=<integer> seeds=<integer>\n  ui         The game UI as the player sees it: HUD, windows and touch controls as text; image=true draws it over the game.  open=<string> close=<string> device=<string> hover=<array> click=<array> wi\n  see        Screenshot with numbered marks on what matters (monsters, hero, townsfolk, loot, spots, level pieces; characters and named objects outside the game) plus a legend: what each number is, it\n# Agent-First Game Engine: Doctrine\n\n## How to read this\n\nRead this as a trusted, capable manager building an engine that you and others will use. We don't need to tell you to lock the door at night, keep company secre\n\n## North Star\n\nBuild a game engine designed from the ground up for AI coding agents (\"agents\"). Every layer is built so agents can develop, test, inspect, and debug it directl\n\nAnalogy: build a fully autonomous fighter jet with no cockpit. Removing the pilot removes the constraints a human imposes on the design. Humans give direction r\n\nOptimize for the best expected overall output, not flawless software. Solve problems when they actually surface, not in anticipation.\n\n## Principles\n\n1. Agent-readable\n   Code, data, and project structure are optimized for agent comprehension over conventions that serve only humans.\n\n2. Agent-operable\n   Every capability is exposed through machine interfaces. Nothing requires a GUI unless no other option.\n\n3. Verifiable without a display\n   Everything can be run, tested, inspected, and verified without a screen or a human watching.\n\n4. Agent-accessible assets\n   Source assets are created in this order of preference:\n   1. Procedural: code and data that generate the asset.\n   2. Text/data: formats agents read and edit directly.\n   3. Binary: only with human approval. Approved: fonts (WOFF2, TTF, OTF).","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"19933fd7-aa65-437e-a4da-e4509b9efb8a","uuid":"1047bd82-4e93-4b8e-b1b6-15f5adc1f3b2","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:59:11.829Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"19:    pub fn as_f64(&self) -> Option<f64> {\n27:    pub fn as_bool(&self) -> Option<bool> {\n39:    pub fn from_json(v: &serde_json::Value) -> Option<Self> {\n42:    pub fn to_json(&self) -> serde_json::Value {\n79:pub fn nested(v: &mut dyn ParamVisitor, name: &str, t: &mut dyn Tunable) {\n131:pub fn list(t: &mut dyn Tunable) -> Vec<ParamInfo> {\n183:pub fn to_map(t: &mut dyn Tunable) -> BTreeMap<String, ParamValue> {\n188:pub fn apply_map(t: &mut dyn Tunable, values: &BTreeMap<String, ParamValue>) -> Vec<String> {\n250:pub fn set(t: &mut dyn Tunable, path: &str, value: ParamValue) -> Result<(), String> {\n257:pub fn get(t: &mut dyn Tunable, path: &str) -> Option<ParamValue> {\n61:pub const PLACE_LIFT: f32 = 0.025;\n82:    pub enum HelmKind {\n83-        #[default]\n84-        None => \"none\",\n85-        Cap => \"cap\",\n86-        Helm => \"helm\",\n87-        Great => \"great\",\n88-        Crown => \"crown\",\n89-        Horned => \"horned\",\n90-        Halo => \"halo\",\n91-        /// A cloth hood with a drape down the back (townsfolk).\n92-        Hood => \"hood\",\n93-    }\n94-}\n291:pub fn event_bursts(e: &pav_core::frame::SimEvent, out: &mut Vec<ParticleBurst>) {\n292-    use pav_core::frame::SimEvent as E;\n293-    let with = |name: &str, pos: Vec3, count: u32, f: &dyn Fn(&mut ParticleBurst)| {\n294-        let (mut b, _) = preset(name);\n295-        b.pos = pos;\n296-        b.count = count;\n297-        f(&mut b);\n298-        b\n299-    };\n300-    match e {\n301-        E::Explosion { pos, radius } => {\n302-            let r = *radius;\n303-            out.push(with(\"sparks\", *pos, 70, &|b| {\n  input      Drive the player: hold a move direction/buttons for N ticks (press =